# Component patterns

## Three kinds of component

| Kind | Knows about | May do | Lives in |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Page** | Routing | Compose features, read URL state | `features/x/pages/`, or `app/` in Next.js |
| **Feature component** | One domain | Call that feature's hooks, own local UI state | `features/x/components/` |
| **UI component** | Nothing domain-specific | Render props, emit events | `shared/ui/` |

A UI component never fetches, never imports a feature, and never reads a store.

## Props

- Type is `XProps`, declared above the component. Destructure in the signature and default there.
- Extend the element or Mantine props you wrap, and pass the rest through so `className`, `style`, `aria-*` and `data-*` work without a prop each.

```tsx
import { Button, type ButtonProps, type ElementProps } from '@mantine/core';

type SubmitButtonProps = ButtonProps & ElementProps<'button', keyof ButtonProps> & { pending?: boolean };

export function SubmitButton({ pending = false, children, ...rest }: SubmitButtonProps) {
  return (
    <Button type="submit" loading={pending} {...rest}>
      {children}
    </Button>
  );
}
```

- `ref` is an ordinary prop from React 19. Use `forwardRef` only when the project is still on React 18.
- Prefer composition to configuration. Accept `children` or named slots (`footer`, `action`) instead of a growing set of booleans. Past three or four booleans, the component wants to be split or to become a compound component.
- Events are `onX` props handled by `handleX` functions.

## Wrapping Mantine

Wrap a Mantine component only when the wrapper adds a design-system decision — a fixed variant set, a default, a composed pattern. Global defaults belong in the theme (`Button.extend({ defaultProps })`), not in a wrapper that re-specifies every style. See `fe-styling`.

## State and effects

- **Derive, do not sync.** If a value can be computed from props or state, compute it during render. A `useEffect` that copies a prop into state is a bug with a delay.
- Effects are for synchronising with something outside React — subscriptions, the DOM, a third-party widget. Not for fetching (that is TanStack Query) and not for reacting to state you just set (do that in the event handler).
- Reset a component when its subject changes with a `key`, not with an effect: `<EditBudgetForm key={budget.id} budget={budget} />`.
- Where state lives is decided in `fe-state`.

## Every async view has four states

Loading, error, empty, success. All four are designed, none is an afterthought.

```tsx
const { data, isPending, isError, refetch } = useBudgets(filters);

if (isPending) return <BudgetListSkeleton />; // shaped like the content, not a lone spinner
if (isError) return <ErrorState onRetry={refetch} />;
if (data.length === 0) return <EmptyState title="No budgets yet" action={<NewBudgetButton />} />;
return <BudgetList budgets={data} />;
```

Put an error boundary around each route and around widgets that can throw on bad data (charts, rich text). A thrown render error must not blank the app.

## Memoization

Do not add `useMemo`, `useCallback` or `memo` by default. Add one when the profiler shows a problem, and say why in a comment. If the React Compiler is enabled (look for it in the bundler config), leave manual memoization out entirely.

What actually helps performance: keep state low in the tree, split large components, virtualise lists over ~100 rows (`@tanstack/react-virtual`), paginate on the server, and lazy-load heavy widgets.

## Accessibility is part of done

- Use the real element. `button` for actions, `a` or `Link` for navigation, never a clickable `div`.
- Every control has an accessible name. Icon-only buttons get `aria-label`.
- Everything works from the keyboard, with a visible focus ring. Never remove the outline without replacing it.
- Colour is never the only signal. Pair it with an icon, text or shape.
- Every input has a label. Errors are tied to their field (Mantine's `error` prop does this).
- Text contrast meets WCAG AA in both colour schemes.
- Motion respects `prefers-reduced-motion`.

## Structure inside a file

```tsx
// imports → types → constants → component → small private subcomponents
export function BudgetCard({ budget, onEdit }: BudgetCardProps) {
  // 1. hooks   2. derived values   3. handlers   4. early returns   5. JSX
}
```

- One exported component per file, and the file is named after it.
- Never define a component inside another component's body. It remounts on every render.
- Compute above the `return`. JSX holds markup, not logic.
- Keys are stable ids from the data, never array indexes for lists that can change.

## Smells to fix on sight

- `useEffect` + `fetch` + `useState`.
- A prop copied into state.
- A `switch` on `variant` returning inline style objects — that is a theme variant or a CSS module.
- User-facing success and error toasts duplicated in every component instead of one place (see `fe-state`).
- A component that takes `data`, `isLoading` and `error` props and only passes them down.
